    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Can you help me draft a quick summary for my CTO? I need to explain the difference between vertical and horizontal scaling in AWS, specifically regarding our microservices architecture. Keep it professional and high-level so a non-technical stakeholder can understand it, but make sure the distinction between adding more instances vs. increasing instance size is clear.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Vertical scaling involves upgrading the capacity of your existing servers, such as adding more RAM or CPU wheels, to handle heavier individual workloads, whereas horizontal scaling adds more separate instances of your application across different machines to distribute the load. For our microservices architecture, horizontal scaling is generally preferred because it allows us to increase system capacity simply by deploying more worker nodes rather than being limited by the maximum upgradeable size of a single server. This approach also offers greater resilience, as the failure of one instance does not bring down the entire service and makes our infrastructure more cost-effective at scale.",
      "source": "target",
