    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a good start. Can you expand a bit on the risks? Specifically, I want to mention what happens when things go wrong—like sudden, unpredictable spikes or unexpected errors in the load balancer. How should I describe those \"unpredictable\" technical failures in a way that conveys the urgency to a stakeholder without sounding too alarmist?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Vertical scaling creates a single point of failure because if the primary server crashes, the entire service stops until it can be patched or replaced. In contrast, our horizontal approach allows the system to handle sudden traffic spikes or unexpected load balancer errors by instantly routing requests to healthy backup instances, ensuring availability without manual intervention. This redundancy provides a critical safety buffer that maintains business continuity even during technical hiccups that would cripple a monolithic, vertically scaled architecture. By distributing our risk across multiple nodes, we transform potential workplace emergencies into manageable, routine recovery events.",
      "source": "target",
